    "narrative": "Our lineup today centers on **Corbin Carroll** (75.15) and **Josh Naylor** (71.72) as our primary drivers for run production. **Corbin Carroll** faces Michael King in a matchup that benefits from a strong platoon advantage, and despite King\u2019s ability to hold runners, **Corbin Carroll** remains our top option due to his high volume of plate appearances at the #3 spot. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** offers elite upside against Tyler Phillips (4.78 xERA), benefiting from a favorable platoon split and a solid spot in the order. **James Wood** (71.07) rounds out our elite tier; his 0.429 xwOBA is the highest on our roster today, and his leadoff placement against Spencer Arrighetti ensures he has the best opportunity to maximize his counting stats.\n\nWe are leveraging significant platoon and situational edges to populate the rest of the board. **Mickey Moniak** (64.13) and **Jose Caballero** (64.51) are both starts today thanks to massive efficiency gains against their respective matchups. **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a +14% boost due to a dynamic platoon advantage against Roki Sasaki, while **Jose Caballero** exploits a high likelihood of stolen base opportunities against Shane McClanahan\u2019s slow delivery. Our most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley**, whose 52.05 score was hampered by a neutral matchup against Jared Jones and a lower-than-ideal order slot. Ultimately, the optimizer prioritized the higher ceiling of **Zach McKinstry** (62.60), who is positioned to exploit a more favorable platoon split against Jeffrey Springs.\n\nManagement must remain cautious, as all listed scores are based on \"Lineup Pending\" projections; the actual batting order could shift these numbers significantly, particularly for **Dalton Rushing** and **Alex Freeland**, who are currently tethered to lower-order spots that drag down their efficiency. While we are confident in the roster, keep a close eye on weather reports and late-scratch news. **Seiya Suzuki** (52.68) remains a risk/reward play; while he benefits from a hitter-friendly park, the matchup against Dean Kremer carries a negative SP skill adjustment that keeps his score lower than our primary starters. Monitor the status of **Max Muncy** (52.99) closely as well, as his value is currently suppressed by his projected #8 spot in the order against Troy Melton.",
